<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lucretia Mott</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1793">1793</a>–<a href="/years/1880">1880</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lucretia Mott was an American Quaker, abolitionist, women's rights activist and social reformer. Excluded with other women from the World Anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> Convention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1840, she formed the idea of reforming the position of women in society, and in 1848 Jane Hunt invited her to the meeting that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-falls-convention">Seneca Falls Convention</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-sentiments">Declaration of Sentiments</a> was written. A Quaker preacher in her early adulthood, she advocated the vote for black people, both male and female, and her home with her husband James Mott was a stop on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/underground-railroad">Underground Railroad</a>. She helped found the Female Medical College of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> and Swarthmore College.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lucretia-mott.jpg" alt="Lucretia Mott" width="330" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frederick Gutekunst / Adam Cuerden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lucretia_Mott,_signed_photo,_by_F._Gutekunst.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lucretia Coffin was born on 3 January 1793 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantucket">Nantucket</a>, Massachusetts, the second child of Anna Folger and the whaling captain Thomas Coffin. Her mother ran the family's mercantile business. At thirteen she was sent to the Nine Partners School in Dutchess County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, run by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a>, where James Mott, her future husband, was a teacher. Staying on as a teacher at fifteen, she learned that male teachers were paid much more than female staff, which stirred her interest in women's rights. She married James Mott on 10 April 1811 at Pine Street Meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, and they raised six children, five of whom reached adulthood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Recorded as a Quaker minister in 1821, Mott travelled widely as a preacher, stressing the inward light of the Divine in every person, as taught by Elias Hicks. After a visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> in 1818 she called for the immediate and unconditional emancipation of enslaved people. In 1833 she and her husband helped found the American Anti-Slavery Society, which as an organisation for men invited her only as a guest, and she led the Female Anti-Slavery Society. In 1838 a mob destroyed Pennsylvania Hall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, and Mott left it arm in arm with white and black women delegates. Her home, Roadside, served as a stop on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/underground-railroad">Underground Railroad</a>.</p><p>In June 1840 she was one of six women delegates to the World's Anti-Slavery Convention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where the men voted to exclude the American women, partly so that women's rights would not be tied to the cause of abolition. In 1848 she and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-cady-stanton">Elizabeth Cady Stanton</a> organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-falls-convention">Seneca Falls Convention</a>, and she signed its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-sentiments">Declaration of Sentiments</a>, though she at first opposed Stanton's resolution on the vote. At the Liberty Party's convention that June, five delegates voted for her as candidate for Vice President. She published the Discourse on Woman in 1850 and in 1866 became first president of the American Equal Rights Association.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Mott helped found the Female Medical College of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> and Swarthmore College, raised funds for the Philadelphia School of Design for Women, and was a pacifist who opposed the War with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and led the Pennsylvania Peace Society. She helped form the Free Religious Association in 1867. She died of pneumonia at Roadside on 11 November 1880 and was buried at Fair Hill Burial Ground, a Quaker cemetery. At her funeral a long silence was broken when someone asked, Who can speak? The preacher is dead. The area around her home is now known as La Mott.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lucretia Mott” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Anti-Slavery Convention in London in 1840 refused to seat the American women delegates, among them the Quaker minister <b>Lucretia Mott</b>, she and the young Elizabeth Cady Stanton, who was in London on her honeymoon, resolved, by Stanton’s later account, to hold a convention on the rights of women.</blockquote><a href="/movement/womens-suffrage-movement">Women's suffrage movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>In the United States the early campaigners <b>Lucretia Mott</b>, Elizabeth Cady Stanton and Susan B. Anthony, all opponents of slavery first, drew on the Quaker theology of spiritual equality, which holds men and women equal under God, and on earlier writers such as Judith Sargent Murray, John Neal,…</blockquote><a href="/ideology/feminism">Feminism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Elizabeth Cady Stanton and <b>Lucretia Mott</b> opened the first women's rights convention in the United States.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-19">19 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantucket">Nantucket</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.28,-70.09&amp;year=1849" aria-label="Nantucket on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Lucretia Mott"><image href="/maps/globes/g300n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4355 4013h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4355 4013h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.28,-70.09&amp;year=1849">What was here in 1849? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
